h&#39;s revenue spread over its nights, booked or not. Booking lead time is the days between booking and check-in, and length of stay the nights per booking, for stays in that month.</p> <p>The typical Garden City Beach listing earns about $3,470 a month, but one property can land well above or below that. The <a href="/calculator">Airbnb estimator</a> gives a range for a specific address, built from the comparable listings around it.</p>  </div> </section>  <section id="profit" class="mg-section mg-col"> <div class="mg-title mb-8"> <p class="font-lato text-[12px] font-bold uppercase tracking-[0.1em] text-[rgba(0,0,0,0.6)]"> Profit </p> <h2 class="mg-serif mt-1.5 text-[30px] font-bold leading-[1.12] sm:text-[36px] tracking-[-0.012em] text-[#1e293b]"> Is an Airbnb in Garden City Beach Profitable? </h2>  </div> <div class="mg-report prose max-w-none"> <p id="profit-summary">Yes, when it's run well. The average Garden City Beach Airbnb took in <strong>$41,223</strong> in the 12 months to September 2026, and run by its owner at everyday costs, about <strong>$2,110 a month</strong> of that is left after running costs, so a home that costs less than that to own or rent makes money. A listing in the top quarter leaves about <strong>$3,610 a month</strong>.</p><p>Cleaning it yourself keeps the $5,720 a year guests pay in cleaning fees, for about $2,590 a month. Handing the listing to a co-host at about 10% of nightly bookings still leaves about $1,820 a month.</p><p>
+These amounts come before housing costs. Where the mortgage or rent is less each month, the difference is
+        profit; a home owned outright keeps it all after property tax, home insurance and any HOA or condo fees.
